config RT_IPC
	bool "Real-time migrating-thread IPC (rt_ipc)"
	depends on X86_64
	depends on RUST
	help
	  rt_ipc is a real-time inter-process communication mechanism based on
	  the migrating-thread model (Ford & Lepreau, USENIX 1994).  Instead of
	  dispatching a remote procedure call to a separate server thread — as
	  the static two-thread model, Android Binder and local sockets do — the
	  client's own thread migrates into the server address space and runs the
	  handler with the client's scheduling attributes.  Only the address
	  space and a small subset of CPU state are switched; the scheduler is
	  not involved.

	  Because the server code executes with the client's priority, the
	  classic priority-inversion problem cannot arise and no proxy-execution
	  machinery is required.  This yields significantly lower and more
	  predictable RPC latency than thread-handoff IPC.

	  The mechanism is implemented in Rust (endpoint registry and policy in a
	  small, unit-tested core) with a thin, arch-specific C/asm layer for the
	  partial context switch.  It adds three syscalls: rt_ipc_register,
	  rt_ipc_invoke and rt_ipc_return.

	  Userspace reference library, demo and tests live under
	  tools/testing/selftests/rt_ipc/.

	  If unsure, say N.
